# Skew 100,000 documents, 640 hot rows, query t0 t2 t4 t6, k=10, WAND
#
# The fixture the Zipf corpora could not be. Zipf term frequencies and
# document lengths are uniform, so every block of a term carries the same
# impact pair and no block bound can ever fall below the threshold; WAND
# reporting zero skips there was honest. Here the high-impact rows are a
# contiguous prefix, so once the heap is full almost every later block is
# provably unreachable.
#
# These are the counters after the block-max seek target stopped being
# capped at the end of the block the pivot was standing in. Before that
# fix WAND decoded all 2,621 blocks and skipped none, at every k: however
# many blocks the bound had just condemned, the jump could not cross more
# than one block boundary, so the cursor landed in the next block, decoded
# it and asked the same question again.
#
#   blocks_decoded   2621 -> 21
#   blocks_skipped      0 -> 2600
#   docs_evaluated     63 -> 63
#   postings_decoded  168 -> 168
#
# The two scoring counters not moving is the point: the same documents are
# evaluated from the same postings, and the hits are bit-identical to the
# exhaustive scan, which is the assertion the test makes first. What
# changed is only how much of the segment had to be unpacked to reach
# them -- 0.8% of the blocks instead of all of them.
docs_evaluated = 63
blocks_decoded = 21
blocks_skipped = 2600
postings_decoded = 168
